{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789836396,"nonce":"vLZA1+5X0UfM4nbAgZzdvWvFmuQ=","title":"Compiling a function of thousands of blocks took minutes","message":"A test file's top-level chunk, thousands of blocks and values, exposed several passes that cost the whole function per element: the SSA builder recursed through single-predecessor chains (a stack overflow on a straight-line body) and scanned every block for predecessors per read; scalar_replace_alloc gathered the function's constants and swept every other block per allocation; each OSR layout computed region dominators as full sets per block (quadratic memory, cubic time); DominatorTree::dominates walked an idom chain as long as the body; drop_insert swept the function to a fixpoint per allocation; and the HIR interpreter panicked with register overflow past 65535 values instead of refusing the function.\n\npm.lua from the Lua 5.4 test suite: 24 s to compile in release, 222 s in debug, before; 5.6 s and 57 s after, and the frontend now splits such a chunk into segments besides.","files":null}]}